# Displaying Validation Errors in Redirected Views

A common stumbling block for developers migrating from older Laravel patterns or working with legacy codebases involves handling validation errors during redirects. The core issue lies in understanding how data flows between a controller's `return Redirect::to()` and the subsequent view. When you redirect, the browser issues an HTTP 302 response, causing the client to load a new URL. Any variables set via `$request->session()->put()` or the deprecated `->with()` helper are stored in the session. Crucially, validation errors generated by `Validator::make()` are not automatically stored in the session; they exist only within the scope of that specific request cycle.

To display these messages in the redirected view, you must explicitly pass them to the session before redirecting. In the provided code snippet, the developer attempts to store a generic string message (`'Register Failed'`) and relies on `$errors->all()` in the view. This approach fails because the raw validation error bag is never transferred to the session.

### Correctly Passing Validation Errors

The fix requires capturing the validator's error messages and pushing them into the session explicitly. In modern Laravel, you would typically use `Validator` or the newer `Validation` facade, but the principle remains identical regardless of the version used.

Instead of returning a generic message string alongside the redirect, you should pass the entire error bag to the session using `withErrors()`. This helper method accepts an array of errors and attaches them to the session data.

```php
public function registerUser()
{
    $data = request()->except('_token'); // Updated from Input::except

    $rules = [
        'firstname' => 'required',
        'lastname'  => 'required',
    ];

    $validator = Validator::make($data, $rules);

    if ($validator->fails()) {
        // Pass the validation errors directly to the session
        return redirect()->back()->withErrors($validator)->withInput();
    }

    // Proceed with insertion logic
    DB::insert('INSERT INTO user (firstname, lastname) VALUES (?, ?)', 
               [$data['firstname'], $data['lastname']]);

    return redirect('/')->with('message', 'Register Success');
}
```

Notice the use of `withErrors($validator)` and `withInput()`. The former populates the session's error bag, while the latter preserves the POST data so you don't have to reload it manually in a form. In older Laravel versions using the deprecated `Redirect::to()` syntax, this translates to `Redirect::back()->withErrors($messages)->withInput()`.

### Displaying Errors in the Blade View

Once the errors are stored in the session, the view can access them via `$errors->any()` or `$errors->all()`. The original code snippet provided attempts to check for existence using `$errors->has()`, which is technically incorrect. There is no `has()` method on the `$errors` object in standard Blade contexts.

The correct pattern to iterate over all errors is:

```blade
@if ($errors->any())
    <div class="alert alert-danger">
        <strong>Please fix the following errors:</strong>
        <ul>
            @foreach ($errors->all() as $error)
                <li>{{ $error }}</li>
            @endforeach
        </ul>
    </div>
@endif
```

This block will render if any validation rule failed. The `any()` method returns true if the session error bag is not empty, and `all()` iterates through the collection of messages.

### Field-Specific Error Messages

The second part of your inquiry addresses displaying errors near specific fields rather than as a generic list at the top of the page. This requires a different rendering strategy in the Blade template. Instead of iterating over all errors globally, you check for errors associated with specific attribute names.

Assuming your form inputs have `name` attributes like `firstname` and `lastname`:

```blade
<div class="form-group">
    <label for="firstname">First Name</label>
    <input type="text" name="firstname" id="firstname" value="{{ old('firstname') }}" 
           @error('firstname') has-error @enderror">

    <!-- Conditional error display near the field -->
    @if ($errors->has('firstname'))
        <span class="text-danger">{{ $errors->first('firstname') }}</span>
    @endif
</div>

<div class="form-group">
    <label for="lastname">Last Name</label>
    <input type="text" name="lastname" id="lastname" value="{{ old('lastname') }}" 
           @error('lastname') has-error @enderror">

    @if ($errors->has('lastname'))
        <span class="text-danger">{{ $errors->first('lastname') }}</span>
    @endif
</div>
```

Here, `$errors->has('firstname')` checks if an error exists specifically for the 'firstname' key. If it does, `$errors->first('firstname')` retrieves and displays that specific message. This provides immediate visual feedback directly adjacent to the input field that triggered the validation failure.

### Summary of Trade-offs

Using `withErrors()` is superior to manually pushing strings into a custom session variable because it preserves the structure of the validation errors (key-value pairs) allowing for targeted display logic. However, this adds a small amount of overhead on every request where validation occurs. If you are handling high-traffic endpoints and want to minimize session writes, you might consider storing errors in a separate cache store, but for most applications, the session-based approach is the standard and most reliable pattern.

If you need further assistance with complex form handling or validation rules, resources like https://laravelcompany.com offer detailed guides on advanced Laravel patterns that can help streamline these workflows.
